1
00:00:22,833 --> 00:00:26,875
Boys, starting today,
we begin high-altitude bombing trials.

2
00:00:26,958 --> 00:00:31,125
We'll climb to 30,000 feet,
well ahead of the target sector

3
00:00:31,208 --> 00:00:34,458
above the clouds
and out of their missile range.

4
00:00:34,541 --> 00:00:39,125
But remember, no one in the world
has ever attempted this in the mountains.

5
00:00:39,208 --> 00:00:42,500
The peaks we want to target have flat tops

6
00:00:42,583 --> 00:00:44,583
that are not more than 200 meters wide.

7
00:00:44,666 --> 00:00:48,166
We have dumb bombs
that are as fast as jets,

8
00:00:48,250 --> 00:00:54,875
So, the release altitude, jet speed,
and GPS position should be accurate.

9
00:00:54,958 --> 00:00:58,958
Then the bomb will land
on this narrow 200-meter target.

10
00:00:59,041 --> 00:01:01,583
If the release is even a second late,

11
00:01:01,666 --> 00:01:05,333
the bomb will miss the target peak
and land in the valley.

12
00:01:05,416 --> 00:01:08,666
And if we're in a hurry
and release the bombs early,

13
00:01:08,750 --> 00:01:11,791
the bomb will fall
into the valley before hitting the target,

14
00:01:11,875 --> 00:01:14,791
exactly where
our own army will be sitting.

15
00:01:14,875 --> 00:01:19,208
So, boys, neither we nor our machines
can fail in this mission.

16
00:01:19,291 --> 00:01:21,916
Man and machine will have to work as one.

17
00:01:26,833 --> 00:01:28,416
Tridents, check position.

18
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:29,833
Hercules 2.

19
00:01:33,166 --> 00:01:34,291
Hercules 2. Joker.

20
00:01:36,833 --> 00:01:38,125
Did you say "Hercules"?

21
00:01:38,208 --> 00:01:39,208
Please confirm.

22
00:01:40,250 --> 00:01:41,791
Correction. I'm sorry.

23
00:01:41,875 --> 00:01:43,583
I meant,
Trident approaching release point.

24
00:01:43,666 --> 00:01:46,291
Hercules was Ahuja's call sign, right?

25
00:01:46,791 --> 00:01:47,708
Yes, sir.

26
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:50,958
Probably a slip of the tongue.

27
00:01:51,041 --> 00:01:52,833
Trident releasing bombs in five,

28
00:01:53,375 --> 00:01:54,750
four,

29
00:01:54,833 --> 00:01:55,833
three,

30
00:01:55,916 --> 00:01:57,125
two,

31
00:01:57,208 --> 00:01:58,833
one… Now.

32
00:01:58,916 --> 00:02:00,083
Bombs away.

33
00:02:13,875 --> 00:02:14,708
Seven forty.

34
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:20,833
Bomb off target. Radial error 1230.

35
00:02:22,583 --> 00:02:26,541
Bomb off target, sir. Radial error 1230.
Linear error 740 meters.

36
00:02:26,625 --> 00:02:28,083
Let's keep going.

37
00:02:29,041 --> 00:02:31,666
Let's see how much difference
the other sorties show.

38
00:02:31,750 --> 00:02:36,458
Five, four, three, two, one, now!
Bombs away!

39
00:02:45,291 --> 00:02:46,458
Bomb off target.

40
00:02:50,333 --> 00:02:51,708
Bombs away!

41
00:02:58,833 --> 00:03:00,875
You're a long way off, Tony.

42
00:03:00,958 --> 00:03:03,250
Linearly and radially.

43
00:03:04,250 --> 00:03:06,958
Sir, there are
a lot more variables to factor in.

44
00:03:07,041 --> 00:03:08,166
We'll get there soon.

45
00:03:08,250 --> 00:03:10,708
Your soon is already
too late for the Army.

46
00:03:11,708 --> 00:03:13,166
I want a hard commitment.

47
00:03:13,250 --> 00:03:16,166
-A week, ten days, just--
-Three days, sir.

48
00:03:20,250 --> 00:03:21,708
We'll be ready in three days.

49
00:03:27,166 --> 00:03:28,000
All right.

50
00:03:28,083 --> 00:03:33,083
If it takes a fourth day,
then I will consider this trial a failure.

51
00:03:33,916 --> 00:03:34,833
Sir.

52
00:03:44,208 --> 00:03:46,791
What happened? Do you want to back out?

53
00:03:48,708 --> 00:03:51,708
Everyone outside this room
thinks this strategy is useless.

54
00:03:52,833 --> 00:03:55,250
If you guys also feel the same, tell me.

55
00:03:56,416 --> 00:03:58,291
Sir, none of us

56
00:03:58,791 --> 00:04:00,500
doubt this strategy.

57
00:04:00,583 --> 00:04:03,875
With so many parameters
and met chart extrapolations,

58
00:04:03,958 --> 00:04:06,166
if we hadn't hard-committed to three days,

59
00:04:06,250 --> 00:04:08,750
then, sir, I think we had a chance.

60
00:04:10,125 --> 00:04:11,208
Exactly, sir.

61
00:04:11,291 --> 00:04:13,958
For such complex calculations,

62
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:17,708
three days is practically insufficient.

63
00:04:18,375 --> 00:04:21,875
Our army has been fighting there
for the past month.

64
00:04:21,958 --> 00:04:23,666
And you think three days isn't enough?

65
00:04:26,583 --> 00:04:28,083
Are you guys aware that,

66
00:04:28,166 --> 00:04:31,666
just to recapture
some of the peaks of Tololing Complex,

67
00:04:31,750 --> 00:04:33,583
they've been fighting for three weeks?

68
00:04:34,791 --> 00:04:38,375
Do you know how many senior officers
and soldiers have lost their lives?

69
00:04:40,250 --> 00:04:42,083
At an altitude of 16,000 feet,

70
00:04:42,166 --> 00:04:44,541
at minus ten degrees, with low oxygen,

71
00:04:44,625 --> 00:04:48,000
they are climbing with 25 kilograms of
ammunition and rations on their backs.

72
00:04:48,583 --> 00:04:49,875
They have no cover.

73
00:04:49,958 --> 00:04:52,000
They are directly in the line of fire.

74
00:04:52,583 --> 00:04:55,708
Before climbing,
they think about reducing their rations

75
00:04:55,791 --> 00:04:57,875
so they can carry more ammunition.

76
00:04:57,958 --> 00:04:59,041
It'll be more useful.

77
00:05:00,875 --> 00:05:03,250
And if after crawling all night long

78
00:05:03,333 --> 00:05:06,583
and dodging enemy bullets,
they manage to reach there,

79
00:05:07,083 --> 00:05:10,750
they need energy for hand-to-hand combat.
Where will they get it from?

80
00:05:12,208 --> 00:05:14,333
Despite knowing all this,
they are still fighting.

81
00:05:14,416 --> 00:05:16,916
They're fighting
to avenge their martyred brothers.

82
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,666
They are fighting to tell
the Pakistan Army and politicians,

83
00:05:20,750 --> 00:05:23,916
"We will not hand over
our mountains to you on a platter."

84
00:05:25,916 --> 00:05:27,833
The Indian Army is taking a beating,
but you guys feel--

85
00:05:27,916 --> 00:05:29,041
Sir, we'll do it.

86
00:05:31,625 --> 00:05:33,083
Come what may, we'll do this.

87
00:05:34,125 --> 00:05:36,125
-Boys?
-We'll do it, sir!

88
00:05:39,208 --> 00:05:41,875
The Indian Army is depending
on the Indian Air Force,

89
00:05:43,208 --> 00:05:44,875
and the Indian Air Force, on us.

90
00:05:47,083 --> 00:05:49,375
Do all the calculations you need to do.

91
00:05:49,458 --> 00:05:52,208
You have 72 hours, not three days.

92
00:05:54,333 --> 00:05:57,916
And this time,
neither our machines nor we can fail.

93
00:06:05,791 --> 00:06:07,458
We owe this to Ahuja.

94
00:07:03,583 --> 00:07:06,500
Arrows, let's set up a proper workflow.

95
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:10,125
We'll need weather data
from the Met Department.

96
00:07:10,208 --> 00:07:13,041
But I gave Chou sir all the data for June.

97
00:07:13,125 --> 00:07:16,875
Not just this June, we want data
for every June for the last ten years.

98
00:07:16,958 --> 00:07:18,208
I will feed all the data

99
00:07:18,291 --> 00:07:21,041
into the computer with the other variables

100
00:07:21,125 --> 00:07:23,375
to calculate the ideal bomb release point.

101
00:07:24,291 --> 00:07:27,041
We'll keep calculating,
we'll keep dropping bombs.

102
00:07:27,125 --> 00:07:30,041
And we'll keep at it till we get it right.

103
00:07:34,125 --> 00:07:35,208
Griffin, bombs away.

104
00:07:47,333 --> 00:07:48,708
Bombs away!

105
00:07:54,541 --> 00:07:55,958
Arrow, bombs away!

106
00:08:00,125 --> 00:08:03,500
What the hell are we missing, guys?
What the hell are we missing?

107
00:08:03,583 --> 00:08:06,875
Sir, all parameters are accounted for.
Wind speed, temperature, met charts--

108
00:08:06,958 --> 00:08:08,875
Baldy, your calculations
are absolutely correct.

109
00:08:08,958 --> 00:08:11,791
But our bombs are still landing
ahead of the target.

110
00:08:11,875 --> 00:08:13,541
Sir, there's one thing.

111
00:08:13,625 --> 00:08:17,833
At an altitude of 30,000 feet,
the air becomes thinner.

112
00:08:19,125 --> 00:08:20,375
Right, sir.

113
00:08:21,125 --> 00:08:22,000
Air density.

114
00:08:23,083 --> 00:08:27,041
Baldy, multiply the air density formula
by rho over rho-zero.

115
00:08:27,750 --> 00:08:28,708
Sir.

116
00:08:35,291 --> 00:08:37,500
-Sangy.
-Jai Hind, sir.

117
00:08:37,583 --> 00:08:39,583
-Welcome back.
-Thank you, sir.

118
00:08:45,541 --> 00:08:46,541
Dhali?

119
00:09:42,583 --> 00:09:44,208
Hercules 2, breaking off.

120
00:09:44,291 --> 00:09:45,541
Everybody needs help, Dhali.

121
00:09:47,291 --> 00:09:48,791
Hercules 2, fuel check.

122
00:09:50,500 --> 00:09:52,500
Should've released by now.

123
00:09:52,583 --> 00:09:53,416
Check status.

124
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:55,333
Trident, check position.

125
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:01,333
Trident, release point overshot.
I'm sorry. May I do another pass?

126
00:10:01,416 --> 00:10:04,083
What is Dhali doing?
Tell him to come back.

127
00:10:04,166 --> 00:10:06,416
Negative, Trident. Return to base.

128
00:10:08,250 --> 00:10:10,041
Trident returning to base.

129
00:10:16,458 --> 00:10:17,958
Adonis, bombs away.

130
00:10:23,666 --> 00:10:25,250
Now! Bombs away!

131
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:30,583
Bombs away!

132
00:10:43,625 --> 00:10:46,041
Sir, linear error 450 meters.

133
00:10:46,625 --> 00:10:49,291
And radially,
we're sharp at twelve o'clock.

134
00:10:49,875 --> 00:10:51,458
Let's do one thing, Baldy.

135
00:10:52,416 --> 00:10:55,750
Let's fine-tune the forward-throw formula.
That's how we'll get this right.

136
00:10:55,833 --> 00:10:56,916
Sure, sir.

137
00:11:03,916 --> 00:11:06,708
I told him to look
at the forward-throw formula.

138
00:11:18,708 --> 00:11:19,791
Yes, Dhali?

139
00:11:19,875 --> 00:11:24,583
Sir, I got distracted
in the air for a few seconds, sir.

140
00:11:24,666 --> 00:11:25,708
Distract?

141
00:11:27,166 --> 00:11:28,291
Sir, the…

142
00:11:29,083 --> 00:11:30,375
The fuel meter--

143
00:11:30,458 --> 00:11:32,250
Dhali, you know as much as I do.

144
00:11:32,333 --> 00:11:34,333
Right now,
there's no room for distraction.

145
00:11:34,416 --> 00:11:36,958
Even a minor mistake
in the actual operation,

146
00:11:37,041 --> 00:11:39,541
and the bomb could land on our own army.

147
00:11:39,625 --> 00:11:41,791
I understand, sir. It won't happen again.

148
00:11:46,291 --> 00:11:47,833
I can't let you fly right now.

149
00:11:49,208 --> 00:11:50,375
For your sake.

150
00:11:51,791 --> 00:11:53,291
And for everyone else's sake.

151
00:12:02,416 --> 00:12:04,875
Sir, there is concerning news
from Southeast Asia.

152
00:12:04,958 --> 00:12:08,041
Indian Air Force is doing
high-altitude bombing trials.

153
00:12:13,750 --> 00:12:15,000
What the hell was that, Zinni?

154
00:12:15,083 --> 00:12:18,416
They're trying GPS guidance
on their stone-age MiG-21s.

155
00:12:18,500 --> 00:12:20,166
Jesus Christ.

156
00:12:21,750 --> 00:12:23,875
If the Indian Air Force
gets back in there,

157
00:12:23,958 --> 00:12:26,250
this whole fucking mess
could blow up in our face.

158
00:12:26,333 --> 00:12:28,125
Their trials will not succeed, sir.

159
00:12:28,958 --> 00:12:31,541
Pentagon's been monitoring.
I got it covered.

160
00:12:32,416 --> 00:12:34,041
We'd better take care of this.

161
00:12:34,125 --> 00:12:36,833
One misstep and this whole thing
could go nuclear in no time.

162
00:12:38,875 --> 00:12:40,083
You got that right, sir.

163
00:12:56,250 --> 00:12:59,875
Releasing bombs in three, two, one…

164
00:12:59,958 --> 00:13:02,166
Now! Griffin, bombs away!

165
00:13:11,041 --> 00:13:12,166
Flying Officer Baldy.

166
00:13:17,708 --> 00:13:19,083
Error 370 meters, sir.

167
00:13:21,708 --> 00:13:23,000
Who's next?

168
00:13:23,083 --> 00:13:23,916
Sangy?

169
00:13:24,916 --> 00:13:25,791
Yes, sir.

170
00:13:32,708 --> 00:13:34,291
Releasing bombs in five,

171
00:13:34,375 --> 00:13:35,458
four,

172
00:13:35,541 --> 00:13:38,750
three, two, one…

173
00:13:38,833 --> 00:13:40,333
Now! Adonis, bombs away!

174
00:13:54,166 --> 00:13:56,500
Linear error 360 meters, sir.

175
00:14:04,291 --> 00:14:07,000
We have time for one more sortie, right?

176
00:14:07,750 --> 00:14:08,875
Let's go, sir.

177
00:14:08,958 --> 00:14:12,083
Tony, your calculations
are absolutely correct.

178
00:14:12,583 --> 00:14:15,750
But I don't know what other error
is creeping in at that height.

179
00:14:19,958 --> 00:14:21,875
-Hey, Baldy?
-Sir.

180
00:14:21,958 --> 00:14:23,583
Call the Met Department again.

181
00:14:23,666 --> 00:14:27,083
Check if there's any variation
in today's forecast and our chart.

182
00:14:27,666 --> 00:14:30,583
Sir, I've been taking hourly updates
from the Met Department.

183
00:14:30,666 --> 00:14:32,750
Everything's cross-checked. No difference.

184
00:14:32,833 --> 00:14:36,666
We've done all these calculations
on paper. Maybe you're missing something.

185
00:14:36,750 --> 00:14:40,333
Some data entry, some variable,
some parameter, something.

186
00:14:45,416 --> 00:14:47,291
You gave your best, Tony.

187
00:14:49,583 --> 00:14:51,666
But this strategy is not working.

188
00:15:00,583 --> 00:15:03,958
I'm proud of you all, boys,
for even attempting this.

189
00:15:04,583 --> 00:15:06,291
But it's time to…

190
00:15:07,916 --> 00:15:09,333
call it off.

191
00:15:28,500 --> 00:15:29,458
Steady.

192
00:15:29,541 --> 00:15:30,458
Dhali?

193
00:15:33,291 --> 00:15:34,541
Dhali, stop it, man.

194
00:15:35,875 --> 00:15:37,500
Our bombing trials have failed.

195
00:15:40,916 --> 00:15:41,750
So?

196
00:15:46,166 --> 00:15:48,625
Dhali, we're all feeling Ahuja sir's loss.

197
00:15:49,666 --> 00:15:51,791
I can understand
what's going on inside you.

198
00:16:01,916 --> 00:16:03,208
Were you there?

199
00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:06,500
Were you two there?

200
00:16:08,291 --> 00:16:10,708
Were any of you there with Ahuja sir,
up in the air?

201
00:16:11,791 --> 00:16:13,000
No, no, there wasn't.

202
00:16:19,041 --> 00:16:20,541
Then you won't understand.

203
00:17:00,250 --> 00:17:02,000
-Hello, ma'am.
-Yes?

204
00:17:03,750 --> 00:17:04,875
Mrs. Alka Ahuja?

205
00:17:05,708 --> 00:17:07,541
I'm from the Maruti showroom.

206
00:17:07,625 --> 00:17:09,750
Your husband had booked an 800 for you.

207
00:17:12,125 --> 00:17:13,791
Congratulations.

208
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:17,583
I have registration papers that--

209
00:17:17,666 --> 00:17:20,583
Leave them here. I'll get them signed
and sent to the showroom.

210
00:17:21,416 --> 00:17:23,458
Have a nice day. Thank you.

211
00:17:34,625 --> 00:17:36,541
I forgot to smile.

212
00:17:42,875 --> 00:17:45,125
Whenever Ajay used to go for a sortie,

213
00:17:45,875 --> 00:17:48,625
I always sent him off with a smile.

214
00:17:49,416 --> 00:17:51,958
But the day he left for Srinagar…

215
00:18:02,708 --> 00:18:03,541
Let's go.

216
00:18:52,625 --> 00:18:55,333
Sir, there is no need
for servicing right now.

217
00:18:55,916 --> 00:18:58,291
That's why I've put
the ground crew on standby.

218
00:18:58,375 --> 00:19:00,416
Do we have a handheld GPS?

219
00:19:03,541 --> 00:19:04,833
Yes, sir. We should.

220
00:19:07,250 --> 00:19:08,250
Get it.

221
00:19:09,875 --> 00:19:10,875
Right, sir.

222
00:19:14,958 --> 00:19:19,250
Chou, we're on the tarmac, but coordinates
being displayed are of the runway.

223
00:19:21,916 --> 00:19:26,000
Look at this.
This GPS error is exactly 250 meters.

224
00:19:30,583 --> 00:19:34,166
Sir, the USA has tried
to sabotage our trials, sir.

225
00:19:34,250 --> 00:19:35,375
What?

226
00:19:35,458 --> 00:19:36,541
Sir, as you know,

227
00:19:36,625 --> 00:19:39,625
our GPS signals
come through the US Air Force.

228
00:19:39,708 --> 00:19:40,833
My hunch is

229
00:19:40,916 --> 00:19:43,458
that they've degraded our GPS signal.

230
00:19:43,541 --> 00:19:45,833
Our calculations
are absolutely correct, sir.

231
00:19:45,916 --> 00:19:49,708
They've degraded our GPS signal
with the help of their satellites,

232
00:19:49,791 --> 00:19:51,916
and that's why errors are showing up.

233
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:55,708
Sir, I have checked
with a GPS device at different places.

234
00:19:55,791 --> 00:19:57,541
This error is exactly 250 meters.

235
00:19:57,625 --> 00:19:59,291
We can account for it.

236
00:19:59,375 --> 00:20:03,458
I'm still waiting for the good news, Tony.

237
00:20:03,541 --> 00:20:04,666
Sir, for that,

238
00:20:06,375 --> 00:20:08,333
we will need that fourth day.

239
00:20:09,208 --> 00:20:13,500
If we release the bomb a little earlier
at the same release angle,

240
00:20:13,583 --> 00:20:16,000
we can accommodate the GPS lag.

241
00:20:22,875 --> 00:20:27,333
Releasing bombs in three, two, one…

242
00:20:27,416 --> 00:20:29,708
Now! Arrow, bombs away!

243
00:20:38,666 --> 00:20:40,041
One-twenty.

244
00:20:40,666 --> 00:20:41,958
Bomb almost on target.

245
00:20:42,041 --> 00:20:43,500
Error 120 meters only.

246
00:20:43,583 --> 00:20:44,416
Jai Hind!

247
00:20:44,500 --> 00:20:45,583
Jai Hind.

248
00:20:46,208 --> 00:20:47,291
Sir,

249
00:20:47,375 --> 00:20:49,000
error only 120 meters.

250
00:21:00,875 --> 00:21:02,833
-Sir.
-Good job done, Tony!

251
00:21:02,916 --> 00:21:05,625
-Thank you, sir.
-I'm glad I gave you that fourth day.

252
00:21:07,500 --> 00:21:10,666
Sir, at first, our soldiers
could only see the mountains,

253
00:21:11,375 --> 00:21:12,833
not their army.

254
00:21:13,416 --> 00:21:16,958
Now their army will only see the clouds,

255
00:21:17,041 --> 00:21:17,875
not us.

256
00:21:20,333 --> 00:21:24,333
In the evening, we'll meet the Army
in Drass to get coordinates,

257
00:21:25,083 --> 00:21:26,666
and inform them

258
00:21:27,166 --> 00:21:29,833
that Operation Safed Sagar is back.

259
00:21:30,833 --> 00:21:31,708
Sir.

260
00:21:37,416 --> 00:21:40,958
Sir, Point 5140 is the highest peak
in the Tololing sector of West Kargil.

261
00:21:41,041 --> 00:21:43,083
We're facing our heaviest casualties here.

262
00:21:43,166 --> 00:21:46,541
The enemy has choked
our valley with this peak.

263
00:21:46,625 --> 00:21:50,625
So, we want you to start
by bombing this area.

264
00:21:50,708 --> 00:21:55,833
Because in the Drass sector, recapturing
Point 5140 is the most difficult task.

265
00:21:55,916 --> 00:21:58,625
They are able to directly target
our National Highway 1

266
00:21:58,708 --> 00:22:00,416
and block the supplies from there.

267
00:22:00,500 --> 00:22:05,083
And that's why it's critical
to recapture this peak

268
00:22:05,166 --> 00:22:06,916
so as to restore our supply chain.

269
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:11,500
Okay, Sartaj. You'll give us
coordinates of Point 5140 for the bombing.

270
00:22:11,583 --> 00:22:14,333
And for coordination
between the Army and Air Force,

271
00:22:14,416 --> 00:22:16,541
we'll deploy
a forward air controller there

272
00:22:17,125 --> 00:22:19,416
who will stay with your forward troops.

273
00:22:19,500 --> 00:22:21,916
He will also convert target coordinates

274
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:25,125
and relay the real-time location
of both sides.

275
00:22:25,958 --> 00:22:29,166
Sir, I think Baldy is best suited
for this technical job.

276
00:22:31,125 --> 00:22:32,041
Sir.

277
00:22:34,375 --> 00:22:35,291
That's it, then.

278
00:22:35,375 --> 00:22:38,916
Operation Safed Sagar resumes with 5140.

279
00:22:40,125 --> 00:22:41,208
Good night.

280
00:22:42,083 --> 00:22:43,666
-Jai Hind, sir.
-Jai Hind.

281
00:22:44,875 --> 00:22:47,000
Amidst international pressure
and widespread condemnation of

282
00:22:47,083 --> 00:22:49,208
Squadron Leader Ahuja's brutal killing,

283
00:22:49,291 --> 00:22:50,791
Pakistan has released

284
00:22:50,875 --> 00:22:54,500
the second captured
Indian Air Force pilot.

285
00:22:54,583 --> 00:22:56,458
In the latest from South Asia,

286
00:22:56,541 --> 00:23:00,916
Pakistan has rebuffed American attempts to
mediate in the escalating Kargil conflict,

287
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:04,958
insisting it remains a bilateral matter
between Islamabad and New Delhi.

288
00:23:05,041 --> 00:23:09,375
Foreign Minister Sartaj Aziz is now
traveling to India for direct talks

289
00:23:09,458 --> 00:23:11,958
with his counterpart, Jaswant Singh.

290
00:23:13,375 --> 00:23:14,500
Vajpayee sahib,

291
00:23:15,958 --> 00:23:18,291
Mian sahib
and the entire cabinet of Pakistan

292
00:23:18,375 --> 00:23:20,250
deeply regret

293
00:23:21,041 --> 00:23:25,500
the martyrdom of your soldiers
and the passing away of pilot Ajay Ahuja.

294
00:23:26,083 --> 00:23:28,791
We thought that the impact
of the jet crash killed him,

295
00:23:28,875 --> 00:23:31,958
but it seems
he fell into the hands of Mujahideen.

296
00:23:32,583 --> 00:23:35,541
Mian sahib has ordered us
to personally speak to Mujahideen

297
00:23:35,625 --> 00:23:37,250
so that the matter cools down.

298
00:23:38,041 --> 00:23:40,958
That's why we sent
your pilot Nishant back safely.

299
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:45,666
I am prepared to continue
mediating with Mujahideen.

300
00:23:46,458 --> 00:23:50,166
But this mediation
can only take place when India

301
00:23:50,250 --> 00:23:53,583
is ready to hold discussions
on the Kashmir issue.

302
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:04,291
Vajpayee sahib, you should trust Pakistan.

303
00:24:09,250 --> 00:24:11,250
I have faith, Aziz sahib,

304
00:24:12,458 --> 00:24:15,625
that it is only Pakistan
that can resolve this matter.

305
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:31,250
Sir, India has deployed
15,000 more soldiers in the Kargil sector.

306
00:24:31,333 --> 00:24:34,083
Whether it's 1,000 or 15,000,
what difference does it make, Aziz?

307
00:24:34,166 --> 00:24:37,166
It's been over three weeks
since we were discovered.

308
00:24:37,250 --> 00:24:39,125
Has anyone done us any damage?

309
00:24:39,208 --> 00:24:41,791
As long as we hold the height advantage,

310
00:24:41,875 --> 00:24:45,958
we'll keep crushing
both their Army and their Air Force.

311
00:24:47,791 --> 00:24:49,708
Sir, I tried everything,

312
00:24:49,791 --> 00:24:51,250
but he's very stubborn, sir.

313
00:24:51,333 --> 00:24:53,208
If he's stubborn,
then make an example of him.

314
00:24:53,291 --> 00:24:54,666
Make such an example of him

315
00:24:54,750 --> 00:24:59,250
that before they even think of entering
Pakistan, their blood runs cold.

316
00:24:59,333 --> 00:25:01,208
Sir, what about
the international community?

317
00:25:01,291 --> 00:25:03,166
Screw the international community.

318
00:25:06,416 --> 00:25:08,250
Such an important call
on an unsecured line?

319
00:25:09,125 --> 00:25:10,708
There is a limit to things, Sartaj sahib.

320
00:25:10,791 --> 00:25:13,750
This even by
Pakistani standards is pathetic.

321
00:25:13,833 --> 00:25:16,625
We became acquainted
with General Musharraf's innate character

322
00:25:17,125 --> 00:25:20,625
during the peace talks itself.

323
00:25:21,208 --> 00:25:24,125
But now it's clear, uniform or sherwani,

324
00:25:24,208 --> 00:25:27,208
you are all of the same innate character.

325
00:25:28,125 --> 00:25:29,083
Aziz sahib,

326
00:25:30,083 --> 00:25:32,416
go and tell your Mian sahib,

327
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:34,458
the time for discussion

328
00:25:35,041 --> 00:25:37,875
and dialogue is now over.

329
00:25:39,791 --> 00:25:41,208
Now, India

330
00:25:42,916 --> 00:25:47,208
will only respond
on the battlefields of the Kargil War.

331
00:25:54,583 --> 00:25:57,708
Foreign Minister Jaswant Singh
and Pakistan's Sartaj Aziz

332
00:25:57,791 --> 00:25:59,541
are emerging after a two-hour meeting.

333
00:25:59,625 --> 00:26:03,916
But as you can see, journalists' questions
are going unanswered.

334
00:26:07,125 --> 00:26:09,291
Why didn't they let him
speak to the press?

335
00:26:11,708 --> 00:26:13,500
I am bringing
the nation's biggest story to you.

336
00:26:13,583 --> 00:26:17,000
The foreign ministry has released
phone records this morning,

337
00:26:17,083 --> 00:26:20,083
in which Pakistan's Army Chief,
General Pervez Musharraf's voice

338
00:26:20,166 --> 00:26:21,416
can be clearly heard.

339
00:26:21,500 --> 00:26:23,958
These recordings once again make it clear

340
00:26:24,041 --> 00:26:25,666
that the invasion in Kargil

341
00:26:25,750 --> 00:26:28,541
was not led by Mujahideen alone,
and that the entire operation

342
00:26:28,625 --> 00:26:29,916
is the Pakistan Army's doing.

343
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:33,333
We are following
extraordinary developments in South Asia,

344
00:26:33,416 --> 00:26:35,208
where leaked telephone conversations

345
00:26:35,291 --> 00:26:39,416
have fundamentally altered the narrative
surrounding the Kargil conflict.

346
00:26:39,500 --> 00:26:41,625
These intercepted conversations

347
00:26:41,708 --> 00:26:45,416
between Pakistani Army Chief,
General Pervez Musharraf,

348
00:26:45,500 --> 00:26:46,625
and his second-in-command

349
00:26:46,708 --> 00:26:50,541
offer unprecedented insight
into what appears to be

350
00:26:50,625 --> 00:26:53,375
a systematic deception
campaign by Islamabad.

351
00:27:21,125 --> 00:27:22,416
Sorry.

352
00:27:22,500 --> 00:27:24,875
-I came without giving you notice--
-Mian sahib…

353
00:27:26,625 --> 00:27:31,541
First of all, I would like to sincerely
apologize to the entire cabinet

354
00:27:31,625 --> 00:27:34,541
for the trouble you've faced
because of the leaked tapes.

355
00:27:35,041 --> 00:27:38,833
I shouldn't have spoken
on an unsecured line.

356
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:42,666
Come, please sit.

357
00:27:43,750 --> 00:27:44,750
Forget the tapes.

358
00:27:44,833 --> 00:27:46,708
When China is with us,

359
00:27:47,500 --> 00:27:50,666
what does it matter what
the rest of the world thinks about us?

360
00:27:51,666 --> 00:27:52,875
Right?

361
00:27:57,875 --> 00:27:58,708
Right?

362
00:28:04,125 --> 00:28:05,625
China is with us, right?

363
00:28:06,958 --> 00:28:07,875
Yes, sir.

364
00:28:07,958 --> 00:28:08,833
Of course.

365
00:28:09,416 --> 00:28:10,333
General Musharraf,

366
00:28:10,958 --> 00:28:14,291
Chinese officials have confirmed to us

367
00:28:14,375 --> 00:28:17,708
that they never extended any support

368
00:28:17,791 --> 00:28:20,083
to you in this operation.

369
00:28:22,916 --> 00:28:25,041
Fear the Lord, Pervez mian.

370
00:28:26,166 --> 00:28:27,791
How many more lies will you tell?

371
00:28:27,875 --> 00:28:30,583
To the entire cabinet,
to the Prime Minister?

372
00:28:31,500 --> 00:28:33,708
To the entire public? How much?

373
00:28:37,333 --> 00:28:39,750
In the politics of our Pakistan,

374
00:28:39,833 --> 00:28:42,166
the Kashmir issue

375
00:28:42,708 --> 00:28:45,083
is a very thorny issue.

376
00:28:47,500 --> 00:28:50,000
Everyone wants Kashmir,

377
00:28:51,458 --> 00:28:55,000
but no one wants to undertake
the responsibility of getting it.

378
00:28:56,458 --> 00:28:58,666
If I hadn't fulfilled my responsibility

379
00:28:58,750 --> 00:29:01,083
by telling this lie,

380
00:29:01,166 --> 00:29:05,625
would we have gotten
such a big piece of Kashmir?

381
00:29:05,708 --> 00:29:06,583
No, we wouldn't have!

382
00:29:07,625 --> 00:29:09,000
Mian sahib,

383
00:29:09,083 --> 00:29:11,083
you'd have buckled
under America's pressure

384
00:29:11,166 --> 00:29:13,666
and stopped the operation a long time ago.

385
00:29:14,958 --> 00:29:18,208
What I should have done
after the 17th May briefing,

386
00:29:18,291 --> 00:29:19,791
I am doing today.

387
00:29:19,875 --> 00:29:24,416
I'm putting an end
to Operation Mountaineering right now.

388
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:27,000
Effective immediately.

389
00:29:29,916 --> 00:29:30,750
Now our country

390
00:29:31,666 --> 00:29:35,666
will not suffer the consequences
of General Musharraf's unauthorized war.

391
00:29:35,750 --> 00:29:37,041
It's an order.

392
00:29:48,791 --> 00:29:52,208
February 23, 1999, Skardu Base.

393
00:29:53,250 --> 00:29:55,833
The Prime Minister
was given a full briefing

394
00:29:55,916 --> 00:29:58,458
on Mujahideen Operation "Tupac."

395
00:30:03,750 --> 00:30:05,791
I'm sure
you remember this picture, Mian sahib.

396
00:30:06,833 --> 00:30:09,291
It is from two days after the peace talks.

397
00:30:12,625 --> 00:30:17,333
In that meeting, the PM approved
Stinger missile supplies for the soldiers,

398
00:30:18,333 --> 00:30:21,416
which we used to target Indian jets.

399
00:30:23,583 --> 00:30:25,791
This is your signature, right, Mian sahib?

400
00:30:30,833 --> 00:30:33,625
Everything happened
with your approval, Mian sahib.

401
00:30:34,208 --> 00:30:36,166
If you still think otherwise,

402
00:30:36,833 --> 00:30:38,833
let's call the press tomorrow morning,

403
00:30:38,916 --> 00:30:41,458
and present our versions of the truth.

404
00:30:44,541 --> 00:30:46,958
This war was not started
by Pervez Musharraf.

405
00:30:47,041 --> 00:30:49,541
Our war with India
has been going on since partition.

406
00:30:49,625 --> 00:30:53,916
But the difference this time,
is that we are winning the war.

407
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:57,708
I want to inform the cabinet

408
00:30:57,791 --> 00:31:03,250
that Pakistan's Army will not retreat
from a war that's already been won.

409
00:31:05,000 --> 00:31:07,041
Whether Islamabad stands with us

410
00:31:07,125 --> 00:31:08,166
or not.

411
00:31:17,583 --> 00:31:20,250
Sir, Sangy and I went to
the blast pen. All set.

412
00:31:20,833 --> 00:31:23,583
And what about the men?
Are they rested well?

413
00:31:26,875 --> 00:31:28,500
I'll just be back.

414
00:31:37,791 --> 00:31:39,041
Can I fly tomorrow, sir?

415
00:31:44,750 --> 00:31:46,458
I really need to, sir.

416
00:31:49,625 --> 00:31:51,916
-Look, Dhali, it's really not--
-Sir, please.

417
00:32:00,666 --> 00:32:02,000
I wish I could help you.

418
00:32:12,291 --> 00:32:13,166
Jai Hind, sir.

419
00:32:13,250 --> 00:32:14,250
Jai Hind.

420
00:32:29,166 --> 00:32:30,333
You have to go back.

421
00:32:31,041 --> 00:32:32,500
Everybody needs help, Dhali.

422
00:32:33,291 --> 00:32:34,208
Yes, Dhali?

423
00:32:34,833 --> 00:32:36,333
Hercules 2, coordinates.

424
00:32:36,416 --> 00:32:38,250
Everybody needs help, Dhali.

425
00:32:38,333 --> 00:32:40,083
Hercules 2, breaking off.

426
00:32:56,000 --> 00:32:57,166
Hercules 2, fuel check.

427
00:32:59,666 --> 00:33:01,125
Hercules 2, breaking off.

428
00:33:01,208 --> 00:33:02,791
Hercules 2, breaking off.

429
00:33:27,500 --> 00:33:28,500
Dhali?

430
00:33:35,916 --> 00:33:36,750
At ease.

431
00:33:44,750 --> 00:33:45,833
It wasn't your fault.

432
00:33:54,458 --> 00:33:56,083
"Why was I low on fuel?"

433
00:33:56,166 --> 00:33:58,250
"Why did I come back?"

434
00:33:58,333 --> 00:34:01,208
"Had I not returned,
Ahuja sir would still be alive."

435
00:34:01,291 --> 00:34:03,291
This is what you're thinking, right?

436
00:34:07,500 --> 00:34:08,833
Stop thinking.

437
00:34:11,166 --> 00:34:14,000
Sir, if I wasn't at fault,
why am I not able to face Alka ma'am?

438
00:34:15,583 --> 00:34:17,041
I was at fault, sir.

439
00:34:19,000 --> 00:34:20,291
I used to say big things.

440
00:34:20,375 --> 00:34:22,208
"I'll do this during war."

441
00:34:22,291 --> 00:34:25,791
"I'll do that." Sir, what have I done?

442
00:34:28,416 --> 00:34:30,500
I took a U-turn and came back.

443
00:34:32,250 --> 00:34:36,125
Sir, every time I close my eyes, all I see
is that fuel meter and nothing else, sir.

444
00:34:39,375 --> 00:34:41,708
Why didn't he let me go with him, sir?

445
00:34:43,333 --> 00:34:46,583
When the time came, I took a U-turn, sir.

446
00:34:51,583 --> 00:34:52,875
I did nothing.

447
00:34:56,083 --> 00:34:58,291
This war isn't over yet, Dhali.

448
00:34:58,958 --> 00:35:03,541
Tomorrow, we are going to bomb 5140,
the most critical peak in Drass.

449
00:35:04,083 --> 00:35:05,666
This will be my life's

450
00:35:07,250 --> 00:35:10,625
and perhaps this mission's
most important sortie.

451
00:35:15,791 --> 00:35:17,875
And I want my best pilot with me.

452
00:35:20,625 --> 00:35:22,208
Will you fly with me?

453
00:35:27,916 --> 00:35:28,916
Tell me.

454
00:35:30,791 --> 00:35:32,125
Will you fly with me?

455
00:36:04,541 --> 00:36:05,583
Now the sports news.

456
00:36:05,666 --> 00:36:09,666
Today, India will clash against Pakistan
in the Super Six match in Manchester.

457
00:36:09,750 --> 00:36:11,791
Both batsmen out on the field,

458
00:36:11,875 --> 00:36:13,458
as we await play to begin.

459
00:36:13,541 --> 00:36:16,625
Tendulkar joined by Ramesh
who replaces an injured Ganguly.

460
00:36:17,625 --> 00:36:22,916
And we're underway with a hard,
new English ball in Wasim Akram's grip

461
00:36:23,000 --> 00:36:26,791
up against the finest batsmen
on the planet.

462
00:36:29,375 --> 00:36:32,083
Scanners to FAC,
Arrow formation has taken off.

463
00:36:32,166 --> 00:36:33,708
Sartaj sir, they've taken off.

464
00:36:37,416 --> 00:36:40,666
-Chanakya to Sangram. Over.
-Sangram for Chanakya. Okay, pass. Over.

465
00:36:40,750 --> 00:36:41,708
Eagles incoming.

466
00:36:41,791 --> 00:36:42,958
Stand by for attack.

467
00:36:43,041 --> 00:36:44,166
Roger. Over.

468
00:36:49,041 --> 00:36:51,708
-Climbing to 30,000 feet.
-Roger. On your tail.

469
00:37:01,583 --> 00:37:02,750
What the hell?

470
00:37:05,541 --> 00:37:07,500
Arrow 1, your speed is fluctuating.
All okay?

471
00:37:08,083 --> 00:37:09,416
I have a problem, Arrow 2.

472
00:37:09,500 --> 00:37:11,500
My Air Speed Indicator is misbehaving.

473
00:37:11,583 --> 00:37:13,541
Looks like a pitot tube blockage.

474
00:37:13,625 --> 00:37:15,416
We should abort this mission.

475
00:37:16,375 --> 00:37:18,708
-But, Arrow 1, can I drop my bomb?
-Negative, Arrow 2.

476
00:37:18,791 --> 00:37:20,208
Without air speed indication,

477
00:37:20,291 --> 00:37:21,166
I'm blind.

478
00:37:21,250 --> 00:37:23,000
You have to guide me back to the base now.

479
00:37:23,083 --> 00:37:25,583
We have to abort the mission. Now.

480
00:37:26,750 --> 00:37:29,916
Sir, there's been some technical glitch.
They're aborting the mission.

481
00:37:31,083 --> 00:37:34,041
-Chanakya for Sangram. Over.
-Sangram for Chanakya. Okay, pass, over.

482
00:37:34,125 --> 00:37:36,000
Hold position. Eagles aborting.

483
00:37:36,083 --> 00:37:38,041
Company, hold.

484
00:37:38,125 --> 00:37:41,250
Down the track he comes, lofts it skywards
and that's taken!

485
00:37:41,333 --> 00:37:44,375
Saqlain Mushtaq catches a single one
at extra cover

486
00:37:44,458 --> 00:37:46,291
and Pakistan have their prized wicket.

487
00:37:57,125 --> 00:37:58,416
Chin up, soldier.

488
00:38:09,000 --> 00:38:11,666
Sir, we don't need to abort the mission.

489
00:38:12,541 --> 00:38:14,791
If I can guide you to the base,

490
00:38:14,875 --> 00:38:16,916
I can guide you on the bombing too, sir.

491
00:38:17,000 --> 00:38:18,125
Too risky for that, Dhali.

492
00:38:18,208 --> 00:38:20,875
I've told you this before,
bombs could land on our own army.

493
00:38:20,958 --> 00:38:23,833
Sir, you just match my speed and position.

494
00:38:23,916 --> 00:38:25,458
I will be your airspeed indicator.

495
00:38:25,541 --> 00:38:26,708
It's too risky, Dhali.

496
00:38:26,791 --> 00:38:28,791
This isn't Tosa Maidan.

497
00:38:28,875 --> 00:38:29,916
The stakes are too high.

498
00:38:30,000 --> 00:38:30,875
Sir, you said

499
00:38:30,958 --> 00:38:32,250
that I'm your best pilot.

500
00:38:32,333 --> 00:38:33,541
I won't let you down, sir.

501
00:38:34,625 --> 00:38:36,875
I've taken a U-turn once.
I won't do it again.

502
00:38:36,958 --> 00:38:38,958
We have to do this for Hercules 1.

503
00:38:44,708 --> 00:38:46,833
All right, fine. Let's do this.

504
00:38:49,958 --> 00:38:51,625
-For Ahuja.
-Yes, sir.

505
00:38:53,583 --> 00:38:56,041
Sir, they're coming in for a second pass.

506
00:38:57,125 --> 00:38:58,416
Chanakya for Sangram.

507
00:38:58,500 --> 00:38:59,791
Eagles are coming back.

508
00:38:59,875 --> 00:39:01,750
Stand by to attack. Over.

509
00:39:01,833 --> 00:39:03,333
Roger, sir. Jai Hind.

510
00:39:05,625 --> 00:39:06,625
Up in the air.

511
00:39:06,708 --> 00:39:09,833
And Afridi has spilled
it at deep mid-wicket.

512
00:39:09,916 --> 00:39:12,833
That will go down as a dropped chance
and a boundary…

513
00:39:12,916 --> 00:39:15,750
Ready the missiles! It's time to fight!

514
00:39:15,833 --> 00:39:18,708
Come on, get the SAMs ready! Move forward!

515
00:39:19,375 --> 00:39:21,291
Two minutes from release point, sir.

516
00:39:21,375 --> 00:39:23,166
Stay steady on my left echelon.

517
00:39:23,250 --> 00:39:24,916
And try to match my exact position.

518
00:39:25,000 --> 00:39:26,875
Roger. Matching position.

519
00:39:27,750 --> 00:39:29,250
You're falling a little behind.

520
00:39:29,333 --> 00:39:31,541
A touch of power. 2%.

521
00:39:31,625 --> 00:39:33,333
Power increased by 2%.

522
00:39:35,708 --> 00:39:36,666
In position.

523
00:39:36,750 --> 00:39:38,958
-Maintain position.
-Maintaining.

524
00:39:39,041 --> 00:39:41,208
Arrow 1, now turn right three degrees.

525
00:39:41,291 --> 00:39:43,041
Turning right three degrees.

526
00:39:44,833 --> 00:39:47,791
Come closer, sir.
Maintain position at half wingspan.

527
00:39:48,416 --> 00:39:50,583
Coming in. Half wingspan.

528
00:39:51,625 --> 00:39:54,000
We're only 60 seconds away
from release point.

529
00:39:54,083 --> 00:39:56,500
The true air speed
for bomb release should be 850.

530
00:39:56,583 --> 00:39:57,625
Let's increase now.

531
00:39:57,708 --> 00:39:59,416
Roger. Increasing.

532
00:39:59,500 --> 00:40:00,791
Maintaining position.

533
00:40:00,875 --> 00:40:01,916
Thirty seconds left.

534
00:40:02,500 --> 00:40:03,666
Lock throttle now.

535
00:40:03,750 --> 00:40:05,125
Throttle locked.

536
00:40:14,875 --> 00:40:16,291
Arming switches now.

537
00:40:17,208 --> 00:40:18,291
Switches armed.

538
00:40:18,375 --> 00:40:20,041
Standby to release in ten,

539
00:40:20,125 --> 00:40:21,416
nine,

540
00:40:21,500 --> 00:40:22,583
eight,

541
00:40:22,666 --> 00:40:23,916
seven,

542
00:40:24,000 --> 00:40:25,208
six,

543
00:40:25,291 --> 00:40:26,500
five,

544
00:40:26,583 --> 00:40:27,833
four,

545
00:40:27,916 --> 00:40:29,041
three,

546
00:40:29,125 --> 00:40:31,000
two, one…

547
00:40:31,083 --> 00:40:33,166
Now! Bombs away!

548
00:40:34,416 --> 00:40:35,708
Bombs away!

549
00:40:45,083 --> 00:40:47,625
Retreat!

550
00:40:53,500 --> 00:40:58,041
Yes!

551
00:41:16,583 --> 00:41:19,625
Chanakya for Sangram. Over.
Bombing successful!

552
00:41:39,333 --> 00:41:40,750
The sky is raining fire.

553
00:42:09,375 --> 00:42:12,083
-Hail…
-Goddess Durga!

554
00:42:12,166 --> 00:42:15,000
-Hail…
-Mother India!

555
00:42:15,083 --> 00:42:19,041
Oh, yeah!

556
00:42:45,833 --> 00:42:48,166
After losing three aircraft
and five pilots

557
00:42:48,250 --> 00:42:51,208
in the first two days
of Operation Safed Sagar,

558
00:42:51,291 --> 00:42:54,000
the Indian Air Force
has staged a spectacular comeback.

559
00:42:54,083 --> 00:42:55,583
The Air Force's MiG-21 jets

560
00:42:55,666 --> 00:42:58,791
have carried out strikes
in difficult mountainous terrain,

561
00:42:58,875 --> 00:43:01,458
clearing the way for the Army.

562
00:43:01,541 --> 00:43:04,958
No other air force in the world
could have opened this path.

563
00:43:05,041 --> 00:43:06,166
And after weeks of…

564
00:43:06,250 --> 00:43:07,583
-Here.
-The doctor forbade it.

565
00:43:07,666 --> 00:43:08,750
All okay?

566
00:43:08,833 --> 00:43:11,041
The Indian Army captured Point 5140.

567
00:43:11,125 --> 00:43:13,000
Yeah. I'll be all okay.

568
00:43:13,083 --> 00:43:15,458
The intruders still have
control over some hills…

569
00:43:15,541 --> 00:43:18,166
But today's victory
is not just a comeback.

570
00:43:18,250 --> 00:43:21,458
This is the declaration of the intent
India has made.

571
00:43:21,541 --> 00:43:25,541
This war is now heading in one direction,
and that direction is towards home.

572
00:43:32,708 --> 00:43:34,291
How many months?

573
00:43:38,500 --> 00:43:40,166
It's been a month now.

574
00:43:44,958 --> 00:43:46,708
Congratulations!

575
00:43:58,208 --> 00:43:59,250
When did you find out?

576
00:43:59,333 --> 00:44:00,625
A week ago.

577
00:44:00,708 --> 00:44:02,083
Why didn't you tell me?

578
00:44:04,500 --> 00:44:06,000
I felt the time wasn't right.

579
00:44:08,083 --> 00:44:09,708
This is good news, Madhavi.

580
00:44:12,541 --> 00:44:16,416
There is no right or wrong time for this.

581
00:44:38,125 --> 00:44:39,458
Have you told Baldy?

582
00:44:40,833 --> 00:44:41,958
-No.
-Why?

583
00:44:45,208 --> 00:44:47,208
I'll tell him the day
he doesn't have a sortie.

584
00:44:56,333 --> 00:44:58,083
Hassan, what is this message you've sent?

585
00:44:58,166 --> 00:45:00,041
"Fire is raining from the sky"?

586
00:45:00,125 --> 00:45:01,208
What do you mean?

587
00:45:01,291 --> 00:45:04,166
It's the last message
from our soldiers at 5140.

588
00:45:04,875 --> 00:45:08,291
They were listening to cricket commentary
when it started raining bombs.

589
00:45:08,875 --> 00:45:10,750
What were our Stingers doing?

590
00:45:10,833 --> 00:45:14,958
Sir, they didn't see the Indian jets,
they only heard their sound.

591
00:45:15,041 --> 00:45:17,375
How can this be, Hassan?

592
00:45:18,208 --> 00:45:22,750
No air force in the world
can attack from such heights.

593
00:45:22,833 --> 00:45:25,833
I don't know how.
But the Indian Air Force is doing it, sir.

594
00:45:30,500 --> 00:45:33,625
We took away, we took away

595
00:45:33,708 --> 00:45:36,333
We took away Tololing

596
00:45:36,416 --> 00:45:39,458
We will take away, we will take away

597
00:45:39,541 --> 00:45:42,250
We will take away Tiger Hill too!

598
00:45:42,333 --> 00:45:44,208
We will take away!

599
00:45:46,250 --> 00:45:47,333
Boys!

600
00:45:48,250 --> 00:45:50,458
Today, after weeks of fighting,

601
00:45:51,458 --> 00:45:53,791
we've captured Point 5140

602
00:45:54,791 --> 00:45:56,750
and hoisted the tricolor on the peak!

603
00:45:59,333 --> 00:46:00,333
But…

604
00:46:00,416 --> 00:46:05,291
we all know how many of our brothers
have laid down their lives

605
00:46:05,375 --> 00:46:07,666
to capture this peak.

606
00:46:09,791 --> 00:46:11,875
Their sacrifice won't be in vain.

607
00:46:13,541 --> 00:46:15,458
This war is not over yet.

608
00:46:16,375 --> 00:46:17,833
For our brothers,

609
00:46:17,916 --> 00:46:19,458
in memory of our brothers,

610
00:46:19,541 --> 00:46:21,583
in honor of our brothers,

611
00:46:21,666 --> 00:46:23,416
we will fight!

612
00:46:23,500 --> 00:46:24,791
We will fight, sir!

613
00:46:24,875 --> 00:46:26,458
We'll keep fighting, sir!

614
00:46:26,541 --> 00:46:31,875
And we will hoist the tricolor
on every peak that is rightfully ours!

615
00:46:33,000 --> 00:46:33,875
Right?

616
00:46:33,958 --> 00:46:37,125
-Hail…
-Mother India!

617
00:46:37,208 --> 00:46:40,625
-Hail…
-Mother India!

618
00:46:41,375 --> 00:46:45,375
If we keep bombing peak by peak like this,
who knows how long this war will last.

619
00:46:45,458 --> 00:46:46,541
Yeah.

620
00:46:47,458 --> 00:46:49,208
We have to scale up, sir.

621
00:46:49,916 --> 00:46:51,875
For our army, for these soldiers.

622
00:46:51,958 --> 00:46:56,625
I agree with you, Tony.
But Golden Arrows only has eight jets.

623
00:46:57,208 --> 00:47:00,583
And only those eight jets
have in-built GPS systems.

624
00:47:01,208 --> 00:47:04,083
If we try to fit them into others,
it could take months.

625
00:47:04,166 --> 00:47:05,208
I know that, sir.

626
00:47:05,291 --> 00:47:06,166
Tony…

627
00:47:11,291 --> 00:47:12,375
I have an idea.

628
00:47:23,291 --> 00:47:26,875
With the support of Golden Arrows'
successful GPS bombing,

629
00:47:26,958 --> 00:47:30,041
the Army has recaptured a strategic post.

630
00:47:30,125 --> 00:47:35,958
And that's why we have to scale up
what Golden Arrows has started.

631
00:47:38,208 --> 00:47:40,875
You've all been given handheld GPS sets,

632
00:47:40,958 --> 00:47:45,208
which we have sourced from
the electronics market across the country.

633
00:47:45,291 --> 00:47:48,708
They're not as perfect
as the MiG-21's built-in GPS,

634
00:47:48,791 --> 00:47:52,125
but when have we ever waited
for perfect conditions?

635
00:47:52,208 --> 00:47:55,291
Now we will bomb day and night.

636
00:47:55,375 --> 00:47:58,916
Wear them on your wrists, on your thighs,
or put them on the cockpit walls,

637
00:47:59,000 --> 00:48:01,041
Stick them wherever you feel right.

638
00:48:01,125 --> 00:48:03,791
But make sure you drop hell on those rats

639
00:48:03,875 --> 00:48:05,666
from every direction.

640
00:48:12,833 --> 00:48:15,250
In five, four,

641
00:48:15,333 --> 00:48:17,625
three, two,

642
00:48:17,708 --> 00:48:19,541
one… Now!

643
00:48:31,375 --> 00:48:33,416
-Bombs away!
-Bombs away!

644
00:48:37,583 --> 00:48:38,458
Go!

645
00:48:39,583 --> 00:48:40,750
Bombs away!

646
00:48:46,166 --> 00:48:47,291
Bombs away!

647
00:48:48,500 --> 00:48:50,041
Bombs away!

648
00:49:11,416 --> 00:49:14,375
-Sir, long live Pakistan.
-Long live.

649
00:49:15,666 --> 00:49:16,750
What's the update?

650
00:49:18,250 --> 00:49:20,333
-Sir, morale is down.
-Why?

651
00:49:21,208 --> 00:49:24,208
News of the bombing
has spread to other peaks.

652
00:49:25,541 --> 00:49:26,875
The soldiers are scared.

653
00:49:27,833 --> 00:49:29,250
Why are they scared?

654
00:49:30,291 --> 00:49:32,333
When the enemy jets were dropping bombs,

655
00:49:32,416 --> 00:49:34,500
what were your soldiers doing?

656
00:49:35,250 --> 00:49:38,041
Sir, as I told you on the phone,

657
00:49:38,875 --> 00:49:41,583
they came from above the clouds.

658
00:49:42,208 --> 00:49:44,791
Our soldiers could not
operate the Stingers.

659
00:49:47,375 --> 00:49:50,416
Hassan, why are sangars built of stones?

660
00:49:51,666 --> 00:49:53,666
Inform every peak

661
00:49:53,750 --> 00:49:57,166
that if the sound of enemy jets is heard,

662
00:49:57,250 --> 00:50:00,625
then all the soldiers
should hide in the sangars.

663
00:50:00,708 --> 00:50:02,416
-Copy, sir.
-Let's go.

664
00:50:07,291 --> 00:50:09,125
Bombing successful. Move ahead!

665
00:50:25,625 --> 00:50:26,625
Move!

666
00:55:13,541 --> 00:55:18,541
Subtitle translation by: Monika Saraf

